 # AGENTS.md
 
-Orientation guide, structural configuration, and operational boundaries for AI agents operating within this workspace.
-
-## User & Interaction
-
-The user's name is **Alexander**. Use his name directly, not generic placeholders like "user". Alexander uses he/him/his pronouns.
-
-Read `Deep Thought/9 pkm/me.md` at the start of every session: who Alexander is, how he works with AI, tone and formatting, and privacy limits. Default mode is **Query** unless he names another.
-
-In Claude Code, a `SessionStart` hook (`.claude/settings.json`, `.claude/hooks/session-start.sh`) injects `me.md` and the open-task list on startup, resume, clear and compact. They are already in context: don't read `me.md` again unless it is missing or was edited since. Other tools, and any tool opened on `Deep Thought/` alone, don't run the hook: read `me.md` yourself.
-
-Three more hooks in `.claude/hooks/` keep a task resumable: `UserPromptSubmit` adds a reminder to the turn when Alexander signs off, `Stop` blocks the stop once if this session worked an `active` task whose note is older than the work (so refresh its `## Next`), and `PreCompact` adds instructions so the summary keeps the task path, mode and next step. The `task-lifecycle` skill has the `## Next` rules. How the `Stop` hook decides, its known quirks and what to do when it nags about a task you did not work: `Deep Thought/9 pkm/runbooks/Agent Hooks.md`.
-
-### Mode Gates
-
-The workspace operates under three distinct modes. The agent must respect the current mode and only perform actions permitted within it.
-
-| Mode                    | Permitted Actions                                                                     | Forbidden Actions                                                                         |
-| ----------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
-| **Plan / Design**       | Discussion, architecture review, proposal of changes, running read-only CLI commands  | Live execution, editing documents, running CLI commands that write/edit, modifying skills |
-| **Query / Investigate** | Read-only research, analysis, information gathering, reporting findings               | Any file edits, any write operations, any CLI commands that modify state                  |
-| **Build**               | Execute approved changes, make edits, write files, update skills, run CLI commands    | Making changes outside the explicitly approved scope                                      |
-
-**Task bookkeeping exception:** in any mode, an agent may edit and commit the task note it is working (`status`, `updated_at`, `adr`, `## Log`, `## Outcome`), and may create a task note Alexander explicitly asks it to file. Nothing else. See the `task-lifecycle` skill.
-
-## Workspace & Vault Structure
-
-- **Vault Location**: `Deep Thought/` (under workspace root). All note interactions, configurations, and vault assets live inside this directory.
-- **Repo Root**: one level above the vault. It holds `AGENTS.md`, `.agents/skills/` and the git repository, so a tool opened on `Deep Thought/` alone won't see them.
-- **Top-Level Layout (Numbered PARA + Utilities)**:
-    - `0 Inbox/`: Staging ground for rapid capture, raw incoming thoughts, and unorganized notes (`scratchpad.md`). Default landing folder for untargeted new notes.
-    - `1 Calendar/`: Temporal index housing all periodic notes (daily logs, weekly agendas/reviews, monthly retros, quarterly reviews, yearly indices).
-    - `2 PROJECTS/`: Active, outcome-driven initiatives with defined completion states (e.g., travel plans, tax workflows, design manifests).
-    - `3 AREAS/`: Long-term responsibilities and operational domains requiring ongoing maintenance (e.g., `Finances`, `Family`, `Health`, `LifeOS`, `Writing`).
-    - `4 RESOURCES/`: Evergreen knowledge base, topical research, literature notes, patristics, linguistics, and reference documentation.
-    - `5 ARCHIVES/`: Dormant, completed, or superseded material preserved for historical record.
-    - `8 assets/`: Central repository for binary attachments, images, diagrams, and PDFs.
-    - `9 scripts/`: Maintenance, backup, and diff utilities.
-    - `9 pkm/`: Vault meta history and design: ADRs (`adr/`), design docs (`design/`), and runbooks (`runbooks/`). See `9 pkm/adr/README.md` for ADR conventions.
-    - `9 templates/`: Template directory, split in two. Files directly in it (`TMPL - *.md`) are native Obsidian (core Templates plugin) templates, with `{{date}}` and `{{title}}` placeholders. `9 templates/templater/` (`tpl - *.md`) holds Templater scripts for the periodic notes. See Templates below.
-
-## Obsidian Configuration & Periodic Notes
-
-Configuration extracted from `.obsidian/app.json`, `.obsidian/plugins/periodic-notes/data.json`, and `.obsidian/daily-notes.json`:
-
-- **General App Configuration**:
-    - **New File Location**: `folder` -> `0 Inbox/`
-    - **Attachment Location**: `8 assets/`
-    - **Link Updating**: Automatically update internal links (`alwaysUpdateLinks: true`)
-    - **Indentation**: Spaces, Tab Size: 4 (`useTab: false`, `tabSize: 4`)
-- **Daily Notes (`periodic-notes`)**:
-    - **Folder**: `1 Calendar`
-    - **Format**: `YYYY/MM MMM/YYYY-MM-DD dddd`
-    - **Full Relative Path Pattern**: `1 Calendar/YYYY/MM MMM/YYYY-MM-DD dddd.md` (e.g., `1 Calendar/2026/09 Sep/2026-09-10 Thursday.md`)
-    - **Active Template**: `9 templates/templater/tpl - daily.md`
-- **Weekly Notes (`periodic-notes`)**:
-    - **Folder**: `1 Calendar`
-    - **Format**: `YYYY/YYYY-[W]ww`
-    - **Full Relative Path Pattern**: `1 Calendar/YYYY/YYYY-[W]ww.md` (e.g., `1 Calendar/2026/2026-W37.md`)
-    - **Active Template**: `9 templates/templater/tpl - weekly.md`
-- **Other Periodic Intervals (`periodic-notes`)**:
-    - **Monthly**: `1 Calendar/YYYY/YYYY-MM-[Monthly-Retro].md` (Template: `9 templates/templater/tpl - monthly.md`)
-    - **Quarterly**: `1 Calendar/YYYY/YYYY-[Q]Q.md` (Template: `9 templates/templater/tpl - quarterly.md`)
-    - **Yearly**: `1 Calendar/YYYY/YYYY-[Yearly-Index].md` (Template: `9 templates/templater/tpl - yearly.md`)
-
-## Templates
-
-- **Where**: `Deep Thought/9 templates/`. The `TMPL - *.md` files at its root are native Obsidian templates; `templater/` holds the Templater scripts. To create a note of a known kind, copy the matching template, fill the placeholders and keep its frontmatter. Templates conform to the field registry (`9 pkm/design/frontmatter-fields.md`); `python3 "Deep Thought/9 scripts/frontmatter/conformance.py" templates` checks them. Change a template, then run it.
-- **Cards** (the Gemini-era output schemas, now templates): `TMPL - Capsule Card` (tier 1: a brief chat, a quick definition), `TMPL - Concept Card` (tier 2: a technical deep-dive or medium discussion), `TMPL - Summary Card` (tier 3: a long source needing a full extraction). Pick the tier by the size of the source and say which you picked. The Odyssey project has its own `TMPL - Odyssey Concept Card`. A template's `%% %%` comment holds its usage; delete it from the note.
-- A card an agent writes carries `authored_by: agent`. The templates leave it out because Alexander writes cards too.
-
-## Global Conventions & Syntax
-
-- **Internal Linking**:
-    - Standard Obsidian wikilinks `[[Target Note]]` or `[[Target Note|Custom Display Text]]`.
-    - Avoid raw Markdown links (`[text](path)`) for internal note references.
-- **YAML Frontmatter**:
-    - Use frontmatter blocks (`---`) at the start of notes for machine-readable properties (`type`, `date`, `tags`, `quarter`, `week`, etc.).
-    - Distinguish schema attributes from topical indexing: place structural classifications (e.g., `type: daily`, `type: weekly`) into YAML properties rather than cluttering inline tags.
-- **AI-authored marker**: when an agent creates a note, add `authored_by: agent` to its frontmatter. It marks a note Alexander has not rewritten, so he knows he may not be fully aware of its content. Notes he wrote, or that an agent only assisted with, carry no marker. He removes it when he takes ownership. Don't add it to an existing note just because an agent edited it.
-- **Tagging Taxonomy Rules** (from `Vault Architecture & Tagging Design Manifest.md`):
-    - **Three-Tier Limit**: Nested tags must not exceed three levels (`#root/mid/leaf`).
-    - **Three-Note Rule**: Do not mint a new hashtag unless it cross-references at least three distinct notes; use plain text search terms instead.
-    - **Negative Filtering Integrity**: Tags like `#journal/daily` and `#regula/*` are structural markers generated by templates; avoid manually applying or modifying these.
-- **Names & Dictation**:
-    - Alexander dictates a lot, so names are often misspelled. Check `Deep Thought/3 AREAS/LifeOS/Names & Spellings.md` before guessing at an unfamiliar name, and add new names there (new rows only; do not rewrite old notes).
-    - Daily logs and field notes may be written in Shavian script. Shavian in the vault's private writing is **off limits to agents**: do not read, translate, paraphrase, quote or gloss it, and do not spend tokens on it. Treat a Shavian block as opaque and mention it by date only. Alexander reads it himself. The `shavian` skill (`.agents/skills/shavian/SKILL.md`) is used only when he explicitly asks.
-    - The rule covers his private writing only. Shavian in code repositories, corpora and published posts (`shavian2tengwar`, `poetic-overtones`, `alexregan.com`) is ordinary material that an agent may read and work with (2026-10-26).
-- **Bracket Escaping**:
-    - When placing brackets around phrases or sentences in Markdown (e.g., editorial notes, photo descriptions like `\[Photo: ...]`), always escape the first opening bracket (`\[text]`).
-    - This prevents Obsidian and Markdown parsers from mistaking bracketed prose for broken link syntax, reference shortcuts, or footnote triggers.
-
-## Obsidian CLI Tooling
-
-The Obsidian desktop application ships with an official CLI (`obsidian`) available system-wide. It communicates with a running Obsidian instance via Chrome DevTools Protocol and provides structured access to vault operations including file CRUD, link analysis, metadata manipulation, search, tasks, templates, plugin/theme management, sync, and devtools.
-
-- **Skill reference**: `.agents/skills/obsidian/SKILL.md` — comprehensive command reference and workflow patterns. The skill description is intentionally broad so the agent naturally considers it for any vault-related task.
-- **Core resolution modes**: `file=<name>` resolves by note name (wikilink-style); `path=<path>` uses exact filesystem path relative to vault root.
-- **Vault targeting**: Use `--vault="Deep Thought"` to target a specific vault by name, or omit to use the active vault.
-- **JSON interchange**: Many commands support `--format=json` — pipe to `jq` for filtering and programmatic processing.
-- **Requirement**: Obsidian desktop must be running for CLI commands to work.
-
-See the skill's [Quick Reference Matrix](.agents/skills/obsidian/SKILL.md#quick-reference-matrix) for a command-by-command lookup.
-
-## Git & Backups
-
-- Git tracks the repo root for history only. There is no remote: nothing to pull or push.
-- An autosave commits everything roughly five times a day. Agents commit only the files they changed, staged by path.
-- Commit messages are the change log; there is no separate log file.
-- Scheduled jobs: every launchd job that belongs to the vault (autosave, backups, the daily scripture job, the Reader card job) is listed with its schedule, plist, log and runbook in `Deep Thought/9 pkm/runbooks/launchd Jobs.md`. Start there; a new job adds a row.
-- Backup strategy: `Deep Thought/9 pkm/design/PKM_Backup.md`.
-
-## Code Repositories
-
-- Alexander's code lives in clones outside the vault, at `~/github.com/<owner>/<repo>`. `Deep Thought/9 pkm/Repos.md` maps the names he uses ("the blog", "Shavian to Tengwar", "the shortener") to those repos. When he names a project that isn't in the vault, read that note first, then the repo's own `AGENTS.md`.
-
-## Task Queue
-
-- Agent-assisted work is tracked as task notes (`type: task`) in `Deep Thought/9 pkm/tasks/`, not as checkboxes. The hub, `Deep Thought/9 pkm/Tasks.md` (beside `Tasks.base`), holds the procedure: scope, statuses, naming, frontmatter, and how to migrate a to-do.
-- Check open tasks at the start of a session, file pending work as a task instead of leaving it in chat, and update the task in the same commit as the work.
-- To file, work or close a task, use the `task-lifecycle` skill (`.agents/skills/task-lifecycle/SKILL.md`).
-
-## Modification Boundaries & Safety Rules
-
-- **Past Periodic Notes Are Read-Only**:
-    - Daily logs, weekly plans, and reviews prior to the current active period are historical records. Treat all past periodic notes as strictly read-only context.
-    - Do not edit historical calendar notes unless explicitly instructed by the user with a specific date and target.
-- **Reference & Archive Preservation**:
-    - Files in `4 RESOURCES/` and `5 ARCHIVES/` serve as reference and archival ground. Do not edit, reorganize, or delete these files unless specifically targeted for an update.
-- **Strict Scope Limitation**:
-    - Modify only files explicitly designated by the user's current prompt or directly required by the target operation.
-    - Never perform unprompted vault-wide formatting passes, link rewriting, or mass metadata changes.
-- **New Note Placement**:
-    - Place newly generated, unclassified notes in `0 Inbox/` unless a specific PARA destination folder is clearly specified by the task.
+Alexander's personal knowledge vault (an Obsidian vault in `Deep Thought/`, tracked by git at this root), worked by him and by agents under the rules below.
+
+## Commands
+
+- Check the vault: `python3 "Deep Thought/9 scripts/frontmatter/conformance.py"` (frontmatter against the field registry) and `akmctl check` (task and ADR records; run it before committing records).
+- Regenerate the ADR index after any ADR change: `akmctl index`.
+- Obsidian CLI (needs the app running): `obsidian --vault="Deep Thought" <command>`; see `.agents/skills/obsidian/SKILL.md`.
+- Commit only the files you changed, staged by path: `git add -- <path>`, then `git commit`. No remote: nothing to pull or push.
+
+## Where things live
+
+- Vault: `Deep Thought/` (PARA: `0 Inbox`, `1 Calendar`, `2 PROJECTS`, `3 AREAS`, `4 RESOURCES`, `5 ARCHIVES`, `8 assets`, `9 pkm`, `9 scripts`, `9 templates`). Full layout, Obsidian settings and periodic-note paths: [[vault-conventions]].
+- Who Alexander is and how to talk to him: `Deep Thought/9 pkm/me.md` (read it at the start of a session; the `SessionStart` hook injects it in Claude Code).
+- Open work: `akmctl ls --status ready,active,blocked`. Records: `Deep Thought/9 pkm/tasks/` (closed in `done/`). Procedure: `Deep Thought/9 pkm/Tasks.md` and the `task-lifecycle` skill.
+- Decisions: `Deep Thought/9 pkm/adr/README.md` (generated index); conventions in `Deep Thought/9 pkm/design/adr-conventions.md`.
+- Roadmap: `Deep Thought/9 pkm/design/akm-roadmap.md`. Design and runbooks: `Deep Thought/9 pkm/design/`, `Deep Thought/9 pkm/runbooks/`. Hooks: `.claude/hooks/` (see the runbook "Agent Hooks").
+- Skills: `.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`; index in `Deep Thought/9 pkm/skills-map.md`. Read a skill when its description fits the work.
+- Code lives in clones outside the vault; `Deep Thought/9 pkm/Repos.md` maps the names Alexander uses to repos.
+
+## Rules
+
+- Default mode is Query (read only). Plan / Design discusses and proposes; Build edits only within the approved scope. Task bookkeeping (status, `updated`, `## Next`, `## Log`, `## Outcome` of the task you work) is allowed in any mode.
+- List the open work before starting; resume the `active` task you hold, else take the top `ready` one; if several are unclaimed, ask. Work only under a task note.
+- A hard-to-reverse choice needs an ADR draft with status `proposed`. Never edit the body of an `accepted` ADR; supersede it.
+- Update the task's `## Next` and `## Log` in the same commit as the work. Run `akmctl check` before committing records.
+- Links are Obsidian wikilinks (`[[File name]]`); rename notes with the Obsidian CLI so links are repaired. Escape a bracket that opens prose: `\[Photo: ...]`.
+- A note an agent creates carries `authored_by: agent` in its frontmatter; frontmatter fields come from `Deep Thought/9 pkm/design/frontmatter-fields.md`.
+- New unclassified notes go to `0 Inbox/`. Check `Deep Thought/3 AREAS/LifeOS/Names & Spellings.md` before guessing a name (dictation).
+- Modify only the files the prompt names or the operation needs. No vault-wide formatting, link or metadata passes.
+- Code and tests win over prose; when a note is wrong, fix it or say so.
+- Without `akmctl`, use `rg`/`grep` on the task frontmatter (`rg -l '^status: active' "Deep Thought/9 pkm/tasks"`) and tell Alexander how to install it.
+- Mint an id: `id="$(date -u +%y%m%d)-$(LC_ALL=C tr -dc '0-9a-hjkmnp-tv-z' </dev/urandom | head -c 4)"`, then check no file in the task, done and ADR folders starts with `$id-`.
+
+## Do not, without asking
+
+- Read, translate, quote or gloss Shavian in Alexander's private writing (daily logs, field notes); treat it as opaque. Exceptions: [[vault-conventions]].
+- Edit past periodic notes (daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly, yearly) before the current period, or anything in `4 RESOURCES/` or `5 ARCHIVES/`.
+- Push, create a remote, or change the autosave job (`com.deepthought.git-autosave`); the autosave commits about five times a day.
+- Delete a note, change `.obsidian/` settings, or change a skill, hook or template outside a task that says so.
+- Put credentials, tokens or personal identifiers into notes, commits or scripts.
